<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rotenburg an der Wümme</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rotenburg (Wümme)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rotenburg an der Wümme is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> built a castle there in the late 12th century to guard the crossing of the Wümme, and it served as their main residence for most of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 the territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rotenburg-an-der-wumme.jpg" alt="Rotenburg an der Wümme" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: bukk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rotenburg_Rathaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
